    {
      "page_title": "The Eight maxmemory-policy Options",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "When Each Fits"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`noeviction`: Redis as a database or a queue. Never lose data.",
            "`allkeys-lru`: the default choice for a pure cache.",
            "`allkeys-lfu`: skewed access, some items permanently hot.",
            "`allkeys-random`: cheap, fine under uniform access, good baseline."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`volatile-*`: mixed instance — durable config beside TTL'd cache.",
            "TTLs become your explicit 'this is disposable' marker.",
            "`volatile-ttl` uses the same sampling machinery, not a sorted order.",
            "So 'shortest TTL first' is approximated, like everything else here."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "The volatile-* trap",
          "text": "If no key has a TTL there is nothing to evict, and the policy silently degrades to `noeviction` — writes fail while memory looks full of evictable data."
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "`allkeys-*`",
                "lines": [
                  "Nothing is safe",
                  "No pinning possible",
                  "Simple to reason about"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "`volatile-*`",
                "lines": [
                  "Persistent keys pinned",
                  "Needs TTL discipline",
                  "Degrades if TTLs missing"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Setting It"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "CONFIG SET maxmemory-policy allkeys-lfu",
            "CONFIG GET maxmemory-policy",
            "# also write it into redis.conf,",
            "# or the setting is lost on restart"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The Eight maxmemory-policy Options (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Before you assume",
          "items": [
            "Managed services often default to `noeviction`.",
            "Verify with `CONFIG GET maxmemory-policy`.",
            "An unset ceiling plus `noeviction` = OOM, not eviction."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Pinning is the real choice",
          "text": "`allkeys-*` gives up the ability to protect anything. `volatile-*` keeps that power and charges you TTL hygiene for it."
        },
        {
